<!--
    kuna GH-866: a gcc -fstack-protector function whose canary check runs
    right before a tail call kept the entry-side canary load, printing a
    dereference of the never-assigned fs_offset input:

        unsigned long v1;  // rax | stack - 0x10
        long v3;           // fs_offset
        unsigned long v4;  // rax | stack - 0x10
        v1 = *(unsigned long *)(v3 + 0x28);
        ...
        v4 = v1;
        return ext(); // tail-call

    The code is gcc 11 x86-64, byte for byte at its link address
    (-nostdlib -static -Ttext=0x401000), from

        t2, t3, k2   -O2 (-fstack-protector-strong is the default)
        w3           -O2 -fstack-protector-all
        t2s          -Os (t2's source)

        int t2(int i, int j) { unsigned char b[12]; b[0] = i;
            b[(i >> 4) % 12] = j; if (b[0]) return ext(b[0]); return 5; }
        int t3(...)  t2 with `return ext(b[0]) + 1;`
        int k2(int i, int j) { char b[16]; for (int n = 0; n < 16; n++)
            b[n] = n * 3; b[(i >> 2) & 15] = j;
            return ext2(b[i & 15], b[(i + 1) & 15]); }
        int w3(int i) { int v[4]; v[0] = i; v[(i >> 4) & 3] = 7;
            return ext(v[1]); }

    ext, ext2 and __stack_chk_fail are small stubs.  t2, k2, w3 and t2s end
    in `mov 0x18(%rsp),%rax; sub %fs:0x28,%rax; jne fail; add $..,%rsp; jmp ext`.
    No call separates the canary store from that check, so copy propagation
    folds the slot reload into the fs:0x28 LOAD and the compare names no
    stack slot; the addrforced INDIRECT heritage puts on the slot at the tail
    CALL then kept the LOAD alive.  t3 calls ext before its check (the
    GH-183 shape, already clean) and is the control.

    h6 and h7 (-O2 -fno-stack-protector) have no canary.  They compare a
    field at offset 0x28 of two structs and call report() when they differ:

        int h6(struct S *a, struct S *b) { long v = a->f;
            if (v != b->f) report(); return g(&v); }
        int h7(struct S *a, struct S *b) { struct T t; t.a = a->f; t.b = 2;
            if (t.a != b->f) report(); return consume(&t); }

    The detector takes any <base> + 0x28 LOAD for the canary, so it also
    strips their report() arm (a separate defect, unchanged here; the
    assertions accept either shape).  The store-side walk must not follow
    such a LOAD: rooted at it, the walk released v and t, so h6 printed
    g(&Stackfffffffffffffff0) with the store gone and h7 lost t.b = 2.

    Stage: P7 regions, stackguard (ActionStripStackGuard): the canary slot is
    also resolved from the store side, forward from the FS_OFFSET + 0x28
    LOADs the compare reads.  P0 assertion: option stackguard on|off.  Pass 1 (default
    on) proves the fix: no canary load and no fs_offset in any function, the
    rest of each body unchanged.  Pass 2 (off) keeps every check verbatim,
    proving each function carries one.  On the unpatched engine #1 and #2 count
    9 (four pass-1 leftovers) and #3 and #4 count 0.
-->
